Note:--->  For @Transactional  :-
               @Transactional annotation is used to manage database transactions,
               ensuring that certain operations are executed within a transaction.
               if due to some reasons error happens afterwards then whole transaction will be rolled back

Let me show you a small code example using @Transactional so it's easier to understand:

### Without @Transactional:
    If something fails, part of the data will still be saved in the database.

java
Copy code
public void saveEmployeeData() {
    employeeRepository.save(new Employee("John", "john@example.com")); // This is saved
    // Simulate an error
    throw new RuntimeException("Something went wrong!");
    // The error happens here, but "John" is still saved in the database
}


### With @Transactional:
    Now, if something fails, everything is rolled back — no data will be saved.

code-->

@Transactional  // This ensures rollback if something goes wrong
public void saveEmployeeData() {
    employeeRepository.save(new Employee("John", "john@example.com")); // This is saved
    // Simulate an error
    throw new RuntimeException("Something went wrong!");
    // "John" will NOT be saved because everything gets rolled back
}

Explanation:
In the first case, even though the error occurs, "John" will still be saved.
In the second case with @Transactional, if there's an error, nothing is saved, keeping the database clean.